This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedanta. Greek mythology is filled with stories of violence, anger, and hatred. Take the tale of the goddess Medea. To distract her father as she fled with her lover, Medea killed her brother, cut up his body, and scattered the pieces behind her. Then there's the god Kronos, who devoured five of his children at birth to ward off a prophecy that one of them would eventually overthrow him. For a land blessed with exceptional sunshine and good wine, Greece's home to a striking number of tales about incest, brutality, and bloodthirsty revenge.
这里是《Hidden Brain》，我是尚卡尔·维丹塔姆。希腊神话中充满了暴力、愤怒和仇恨的故事。以美狄亚女神的传说为例。为了在与情人私奔时分散父亲的注意力，美狄亚杀死了自己的弟弟，将他的尸体切碎，并把碎片抛在身后。还有克洛诺斯神，他在孩子出生时就吞食了五个孩子，以抵御一个关于他们中有人最终会推翻他的预言。对于一片拥有充足阳光和美酒的土地来说，希腊却有着惊人数量关于乱伦、残暴和嗜血复仇的故事。

2
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Thousands of years ago, the Greek philosopher Aristotle had a theory of why this was. In poetics, he suggested that the goal of art was something he called, catharsis. Writing, watching, and reenacting tragedy was good for the soul. It was a form of healing. The idea that stories can be a form of therapy has carried over into modern times. Sigmund Freud explored the idea.
几千年前，希腊哲学家亚里士多德对这种现象有一个理论。在《诗学》中，他提出艺术的目标是他所称的“宣泄”。写作、观看和重演悲剧对灵魂有益。这是一种治愈形式。故事可以作为一种治疗手段的观点一直延续到现代。西格蒙德·弗洛伊德曾探讨过这一理念。

3
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Psychoanalysis involves understanding the self as a series of stories. After Freud fell out of favor, researchers and counselors have continued to explore the role that stories play in our mental wellbeing. This week on Hidden Brain, how understanding the stories of our lives and rewriting them can change us forever.
